  now(), now(), 4, false, 'Football weekend: the big U19 and U17 national clashes, and the Coupe de France for the seniors', 'On 14 and 15 November, round 11 of France''s national U19 and U17 leagues brings several top-of-the-table clashes: PSG-Valenciennes, Annecy-Montfermeil, Nice-Marseille, Joinville-Sarcelles… And for the seniors, the Coupe de France 7th round goes on. Passe D picks the games not to miss.', '<p>While the big names are away on international duty, the youngsters keep playing. This weekend, 14 and 15 November, <strong>round 11</strong> of France''s national U19 and U17 leagues offers several real clashes at the top. And for the seniors, the <strong>Coupe de France 7th round</strong>, which we previewed on Tuesday, is in full swing. Passe D tells you where to look.</p>
<h2>U19 National: top-of-the-table duels</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Paris SG – Valenciennes</strong> (group A), Sunday at 2.30pm. The clash of the weekend: Valenciennes (2nd) and PSG (3rd) both have 19 points. VAFC arrive in form after beating leaders Caen (1-0).</li>
<li><strong>Caen – Quevilly-Rouen</strong> (group A), Sunday at 2.30pm. The Normandy leaders (24 points) need to bounce back quickly from their defeat in Valenciennes.</li>
<li><strong>Annecy – Montfermeil</strong> (group B), Sunday at 2.30pm. 2nd (20 points) host 4th (17 points), with Troyes to keep in sight.</li>
<li><strong>Troyes – Paris FC</strong> (group B), Sunday. Unbeaten leaders Troyes (7 wins, 3 draws, 24 points) host Paris FC.</li>
<li><strong>Nantes – Stade Briochin</strong> (group C), Sunday. Nantes, 4th, are three points behind leaders Angers.</li>
<li><strong>Rennes – Stade Poitevin</strong> and <strong>Laval – TA Rennes</strong> (group C), Saturday at 2.30pm.</li>
<li><strong>Nice – Marseille</strong> (group D), Sunday at 2.30pm. Leaders Marseille travel to their great southern rivals. Behind them, Monaco (2nd, three points off OM) host Bastia.</li>
<li>Also in group D: <strong>Bastia Borgo – Béziers</strong> on Saturday at 1pm, and <strong>Saint-Étienne – Cap d''Ail</strong> on Sunday at 11am.</li>
</ul>
<h2>U17 National: PSG in charge, clashes everywhere</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Brétigny – Paris SG</strong> (group A), Sunday at 2.30pm, at the Stade Auguste-Delaune. PSG are untouchable: 9 wins, 1 draw, 33 goals scored and only 4 conceded. They lead by 7 points.</li>
<li><strong>Quevilly-Rouen – Caen</strong> (group A), Sunday. The runners-up (21 points) against the best attack behind PSG (25 goals).</li>
<li><strong>Joinville – Sarcelles</strong> (group B), Sunday at 2.30pm. Leaders (20 points) against 3rd (19 points). The tightest game of the weekend.</li>
<li><strong>Montfermeil – Red Star</strong> (group B), Sunday at 2.30pm. Red Star (2nd, 20 points) can go top.</li>
<li><strong>Metz – Thionville</strong> (group B), Sunday at 2.30pm. A Moselle derby, with Metz (4th, 18 points) lurking. Also on: Aubervilliers – Lens and Lille – Drancy.</li>
<li><strong>Troyes – Grenoble</strong> (group C), Sunday. Grenoble have just beaten Lyon (1-0). Sochaux host FC Lyon, and Nancy travel to Torcy.</li>
<li><strong>Istres – Nice</strong> (group D), Saturday at 2.30pm, and <strong>Nîmes – AC Ajaccio</strong>, Saturday at 1.30pm. The group''s two joint leaders play on the same day. On Sunday, Bastia host Saint-Étienne (10.30am) and Valence welcome Monaco.</li>
<li><strong>Stade Briochin – Nantes</strong> (group F), Sunday. Nantes (2nd) must win to keep leaders Rennes, 8 points clear, in sight.</li>
</ul>
<p>Note: in group E, Marseille, top with 9 wins from 9 games, do not play this weekend. Several of their players are on international duty, and the FFF has moved their game against Burel FC to 3 January.</p>
<h2>France''s youth teams in action too</h2>
<p>The best youngsters are with the national teams. On Saturday, France <strong>U19</strong> face the Republic of Ireland at 6pm at the Roudourou in Guingamp, in Euro 2027 qualifying. France <strong>U17</strong> play Luxembourg in Armenia on Saturday morning.</p>
<h2>Seniors: the Coupe de France goes on</h2>
<p>The 7th round began on Thursday night. In Chambéry, the R1 amateurs held Grenoble (L2) for 75 minutes before cracking: <strong>Moussa Djitté</strong> then <strong>Yohann Magnin</strong> sent GF38 through (0-2). The Savoy side go out with their heads held high.</p>
<p>The whole weekend is still to come. The ties not to miss:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Friday 8pm:</strong> Dijon – Orléans (L3), and Saint-Philbert-de-Grand-Lieu (N2) – La Roche-sur-Yon (L3).</li>
<li><strong>Saturday 2pm:</strong> Tourcoing (R1) – Reims (L2), Versailles (L3) – Rodez (L2), and Balagne (N2) – Montpellier (L2).</li>
<li><strong>Saturday 2.30pm:</strong> Boulay (R1) – Sochaux (L2).</li>
<li><strong>Saturday 4pm:</strong> Choisy-au-Bac (R2) – Caen (L3), and Fontenay-le-Comte (N2) – Clermont (L2).</li>
<li><strong>Saturday 5pm:</strong> Firminy (R1) – Annecy (L2), and Tinténiac-Saint-Domineuc (R1) – Concarneau (L3).</li>
<li><strong>Saturday evening:</strong> Béthune (R1) – Nancy (L2), Lourdes (R1) – Pau (L2), Lannion (N2) – Guingamp (L2), Bressuire (R1) – Challans (N2), and Cambrai Amérique (R3) – Amiens (L3) at 8pm, five divisions apart.</li>
<li><strong>Sunday:</strong> Châteaubourg (R1) – Nantes (L2) in Vitré, Saint-Renan (R1) – Laval (L2), Saint-Dié (R1) – Metz (L2), Paris 13 Atletico (L3) – Red Star (L2), little Violaines (district D2) against Vermelles (R3), and to close, Rumilly-Vallières (N1) – Saint-Étienne (L2) at 3pm in Annecy.</li>
</ul>
<p><em>R1, R2, R3 are regional leagues (6th to 8th tiers); district leagues are below.</em></p>
<p>Youngsters fighting for top spot, amateurs dreaming of a giant-killing: this is all the football we love. Passe D will bring you the weekend''s results. ⚽</p>
